{
  "family": "premiseFloatDigits",
  "premise": "host-float-digits",
  "description": "The float-digits premise. A finite double is written with the SHORTEST decimal digits that read back to the same double, laid out by the canonical float rule (WIRE_FORMAT section 2 rule 5): fixed point when the magnitude is at least 1e-4 and below 1e17, otherwise the digits in exponent form (`1E-05`, `1.8446744073709552E+19`: a capital E, a sign, at least two exponent digits), and `-0` collapsed to `0`. Each vector gives `bits`, the IEEE 754 binary64 bit pattern as 16 lower-case hex digits, and `canonical`, the text the canonical writer gives it; the export has checked that every `canonical` reads back to `bits` (zero for the negative zero). The edges are named; `sample/*` vectors are drawn by SplitMix64 from four strata (any finite bit pattern, short decimals, subnormals, integers past 2^53). A host reproduces `canonical` from `bits` for every vector, and reads every `canonical` back to `bits`.",
